<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    nexus-1vc0n: index the read path the new bulk owner-agnostic file_path
    lookup (CatalogRepository.documentsByFilePaths, POST /v1/catalog/list_by_file_paths)
    depends on — ``file_path = ANY(?)`` over every live row, tenant-scoped.

    catalog-001-baseline indexed source_uri (idx_catalog_documents_source_uri)
    and collection (idx_catalog_documents_collection) for exactly this shape
    of exact-match read, both LEADING with tenant_id (every RLS-scoped query
    here filters by tenant first) — but never file_path itself: the
    single-path reads (documentsByFilePath, documentsByOwnerAndFilePath)
    predate this bead and were evidently fast enough unindexed at their call
    volume (one row per per-document write, never a scan trigger). The
    batched registrar this bead exists for changes that: one register_many
    pass now issues ONE documentsByFilePaths call per batch's misses
    (chunked at 300 paths client-side, src/nexus/db/limits.py
    QUOTAS.MAX_RECORDS_PER_WRITE), so a cold index of a large repo runs this
    query thousands of times, each scanning catalog_documents in full
    without an index on file_path. This index follows the same
    (tenant_id, X) leading-column convention as its two catalog-001
    siblings.

    BUILD-WINDOW DECISION: plain CREATE INDEX, not CONCURRENTLY — deliberate,
    same family of reasoning as vectors-003 (populated-table case), a
    DIFFERENT one than vectors-002 (pre-ETL empty tables). catalog_documents
    is genuinely populated wherever this ships. Basis: measured on THIS
    box's own local engine via ``mcp__plugin_conexus_nexus-catalog__stats``
    (2026-09-27) — 23,299 live catalog_documents rows, order 10^4, one
    tenant. A plain CREATE INDEX takes a SHARE lock on the table for the
    duration of the build — blocking writes, not reads — and a narrow
    two-column btree over 10^4 rows is the SAME shape vectors-003 already
    measured "seconds" at 10^5-10^6, so the window here is smaller still,
    sub-second. Liquibase runs the whole changelog in the boot-time
    migration window before the engine serves traffic (same precondition
    vectors-003 relies on), so even that sub-second SHARE lock blocks
    nothing live. This box's count is a development install, not
    production's — the actual production catalog_documents cardinality (and
    therefore the real lock duration) is confirmed at deploy time by the
    Liquibase walk rehearsal against a PITR fork of production (AGENTS.md
    § Engine-service release, ``deploy/RESTORE.md`` in the conexus repo),
    which this changeset rides like every other changeset-carrying engine
    tag. If a future store is ever large enough that this stops being
    negligible, build it manually as CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY outside
    Liquibase, same escape hatch vectors-003 names.

    PARTIAL, not full: ``WHERE deleted_at IS NULL`` matches the read's own
    tombstone filter exactly (documentsByFilePaths, documentsByFilePath,
    documentsByOwnerAndFilePath, documentsBySourceUri all carry the same
    predicate) — a tombstoned row can never be a hit, so excluding it keeps
    the index smaller and lets Postgres's planner recognise the index-only
    match without re-checking visibility against dead rows.

    NOT unique — catalog-016's own header already settled this for the
    single-path form and the reasoning is unchanged for the batch form:
    file_path is repo-ROOT-relative, so two owners legitimately share
    "README.md". A unique index would refuse the exact normal steady state
    nexus-yzij1 exists to accommodate.

    catalog-040 is reserved for a different, already-in-flight bead; this is
    catalog-041, the first genuinely untouched number past it, matching
    catalog-039's own precedent for numbering past a reserved/skipped id.

    DATA EFFECT: none — CREATE INDEX only, no row is read, written, or
    otherwise touched. No FORCE-RLS toggle needed: unlike catalog-016-1's
    UNIQUE index (whose creation validates the preceding dedup), a plain
    non-unique index never scans for constraint violations across tenants
    and needs no BYPASSRLS to complete correctly.

    This whole header, this paragraph included, is a top-level XML comment
    OUTSIDE the changeSet body — the same placement vectors-002's and
    vectors-003's own BUILD-WINDOW DECISION paragraphs use, and the reason
    editing it later is checksum-neutral: Liquibase's changeset checksum is
    computed only from the <sql>/structured-change body inside a changeSet
    (data_effect_lint.py's own docstring; it explicitly excludes <comment>,
    <preConditions>, <rollback> and failOnError, and this text sits one
    level further out than even those). This changeset has not shipped
    anywhere yet, so no deployed cluster's checksum is at stake either way;
    the note is here for whoever next edits this file's header.
-->
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.4.xsd">

    <changeSet id="catalog-041-1" author="nexus-1vc0n">
        <comment>Partial index on catalog_documents(file_path) for live rows, accelerating the new batch owner-agnostic file_path lookup (list_by_file_paths). DATA EFFECT: none -- CREATE INDEX only.</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="true">
CREATE INDEX idx_catalog_documents_file_path_live
    ON nexus.catalog_documents (tenant_id, file_path)
 WHERE deleted_at IS NULL;
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql>DROP INDEX IF EXISTS nexus.idx_catalog_documents_file_path_live;</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
